AAR stands for Absolute Accuracy Rule. Now most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.780 --> 00:04:17.700
piano beginners I see practice piano something&nbsp;
like this. This is a bad way to practice piano.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:27.120
This is something what I like to call "gambling",&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.120 --> 00:04:34.440
which means you are basically executing notes&nbsp;
without this kind of deep thought and complete&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.440 --> 00:04:39.000
awareness of what you're actually doing on&nbsp;
the piano. You're simply just kind of, uh,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:44.100
trying your luck and gambling with your notes&nbsp;
and hopefully trying to execute the correct&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.100 --> 00:04:50.580
note. And you sort of try 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8 9&nbsp;
times without that sort of slow down small&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.580 --> 00:04:56.160
little sections and thinking very deeply. What I&nbsp;
teach my students to do and they get incredible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.160 --> 00:05:03.600
results in just five minutes of practice is this&nbsp;
Absolute Accuracy Rule where they 100 prioritize&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.600 --> 00:05:08.700
the right notes. So you're allowed to throw a&nbsp;
rhythm and the sense of pulse out the window but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.700 --> 00:05:12.180
